// A `unique`-bodied spec whose claim is about a linked external. `double` has no
// body here — it arrives from `spec_linked_extern_mathlib.inf`, spliced in by
// the static-merge linker under the name section symbol `mathlib::double`.
//
// It is the `unique` counterpart of `spec_linked_exists.inf` and not a
// duplicate of it: the two kinds are gathered into different partitions and
// consumed by different predicates, so a merged ordinal reaching a
// `ValidExistsSpec` payload says nothing about one reaching a
// `ValidUniqueSpec` payload.
//
// Shaped the way it is because the corpus runs parse → type-check → codegen
// with NO analysis, so every constraint is carried by the fixture itself:
// the obligation is non-vacuous (P010 rejects a body whose claims collapse to
// the vacuous `HA_true`); the body neither declares a return type nor contains
// `return` (the verifier reduces the retained body without an enclosing
// activation frame); and the single `@` is NAMED — `unique` compares
// source-visible exit states, so an anonymous choice, which no
// `reach_visible_locs` slot could carry, is rejected as P012.
//
// The obligation must also be TRUE at EVERY entry: `unique_spec_holds_at`
// demands a non-empty singleton observation set for every typed entry value.
// The body is parameterless, so that quantifier is discharged trivially, and
// the filter pins the named choice to exactly one value — the shape whose
// obligation `ValidUniqueSpec` is designed to state. `double(3)` is `6`, so the
// one surviving choice satisfies the claim.
//
// No data segment: the merged module built from this one is compiled by `coqc`,
// and the corpus rejects a data segment in any module it gates.

external fn double(x: i32) -> i32;
use { double } from mathlib;

pub fn twice(x: i32) -> i32 {
  return double(x);
}

spec UniqueExtern {
  fn one_choice() unique {
    let x: i32 = @;
    assume {
      assert(x == 3);
    }
    assert(double(x) == 6);
  }
}
